WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:02.568
-Our next guest
is an Emmy-winning

00:00:02.568 --> 00:00:03.936
actress and comedian
you know from her

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.638
critically acclaimed series
"Crazy Ex-Girlfriend."

00:00:05.638 --> 00:00:08.675
Her Off-Broadway show,
"Death, Let Me Do My Show,"

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.943
opens at the Orpheum Theatre
in New York, December 7th.

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:13.078
She also stars
in the Max series "Julia."

00:00:13.078 --> 00:00:15.348
New episodes premiere Thursdays.
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:17.183
-You got any advice
for an old girl

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.319
navigating the West Coast?

00:00:19.319 --> 00:00:24.023
-Well, it usually
goes one of two ways.

00:00:24.023 --> 00:00:27.894
If you're a hit, then you grow.

00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:31.130
Not just your audience,
but you, your power.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:33.399
And you feel it,
and it feels good

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:36.035
and it feels permanent.

00:00:36.035 --> 00:00:37.570
But that's a trap,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.973
because nothing in L.A.
is ever permanent.

00:00:39.973 --> 00:00:40.973
-Please welcome back
to the show

00:00:40.973 --> 00:00:43.009
our friend Rachel Bloom,
everybody.

00:00:43.009 --> 00:00:44.744
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:56.523 --> 00:00:58.190
Welcome back.
-Wow!

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:01.461
-It is so good to see you again.
-It's so good to be here.

00:01:01.461 --> 00:01:03.630
-This is such a weird night
for you to be here,

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:06.599
because you actually
met Michael Shannon before.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:09.369
You were on the show
"Lip Sync Battle" together.

00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:11.437
-Yep.
-You took him on head-to-head.

00:01:11.437 --> 00:01:13.873
He-lip synched an R.E.M. song,
which is --

00:01:13.873 --> 00:01:15.240
He's gonna sing one later.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:16.375
"End of the World
as We Know It."

00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:18.310
And he defeated you?
-Yes. I lost to him.

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.080
To be fair, I was on
"Lip Sync Battle"

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:23.682
with him before my show
had premiered.

00:01:23.682 --> 00:01:25.684
So to the audience
watching "Lip Sync Battle,"

00:01:25.684 --> 00:01:29.322
it was a famous person
and some unknown woman.

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:30.790
-Yeah.

00:01:30.790 --> 00:01:32.592
-They might as well have, like,
picked me off of the street,

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.227
and they were like,
"Oh, good for her.

00:01:34.227 --> 00:01:35.661
She was working
at that restaurant,

00:01:35.661 --> 00:01:36.796
and they found her."

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:39.264
Like, they
truly had no idea who I was.

00:01:39.264 --> 00:01:41.500
So, look, he was great,
but it wasn't a fair fight.

00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:42.869
I'll say that.
-Okay, great.

00:01:42.869 --> 00:01:44.603
You know, today, who knows
how the vote would go?

00:01:44.603 --> 00:01:45.838
-But here's the thing.

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:48.174
This is not the first time
I've come in second

00:01:48.174 --> 00:01:50.309
to Michael Shannon
doing R.E.M. on a TV show.

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:51.744
-Yeah, but if it happens
a third time,

00:01:51.744 --> 00:01:53.112
something weird is going on.

00:01:53.112 --> 00:01:54.380
I was saying earlier,

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.015
this is a fantastic poster
for your show.

00:01:56.015 --> 00:01:57.383
-It's a great --
-It is a great --

00:01:57.383 --> 00:02:00.854
This is a show you planned
to do in 2020.

00:02:00.854 --> 00:02:02.588
COVID obviously interfered.

00:02:02.588 --> 00:02:05.257
You did a run in the fall and
and now you're doing it again.

00:02:05.257 --> 00:02:06.725
Very personal show.

00:02:06.725 --> 00:02:09.228
What sort of set you
on the path of doing this?

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.097
-Yeah, so, I was planning to do

00:02:11.097 --> 00:02:15.501
a big comedy -- musical-comedy
special and show in 2020

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.570
and I was planning it in 2019.

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:20.740
I gave birth in late March 2020.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:24.176
A bunch of sad things
happened around that.

00:02:24.176 --> 00:02:28.181
I was in my office, where I had
written all of the bullet points

00:02:28.181 --> 00:02:32.051
for my planned stand-up
musical show with my daughter,

00:02:32.051 --> 00:02:33.519
in the middle of a pandemic.

00:02:33.519 --> 00:02:35.788
I was grieving my friend,
who had passed away

00:02:35.788 --> 00:02:37.223
a week
after my daughter was born,

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.992
and I was looking at these
bullet points for this special.

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:42.128
And when you look at bullet
points of a stand-up set,

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:45.365
nothing makes you feel worse
about yourself as a comedian.

00:02:45.365 --> 00:02:47.333
Like, the bullet points --
Basically, bullet points,

00:02:47.333 --> 00:02:50.036
it's a way that you remind
yourself, so it's one word,

00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:51.771
where it's like
"pencil," "booger."

00:02:51.771 --> 00:02:54.240
One of them was like "old-lady
pop song?" -- question mark.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:55.775
Like, I didn't even
have enough confidence

00:02:55.775 --> 00:02:57.143
to not put a question mark.

00:02:57.143 --> 00:03:00.747
And I -- "Old-lady pop song"
was pretty good, though.

00:03:00.747 --> 00:03:02.882
It was -- The idea --
It was the idea that

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:04.817
all of our pop stars
are so young,

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.953
but why should we rely
on young people

00:03:06.953 --> 00:03:08.287
to tell us about our culture?

00:03:08.287 --> 00:03:09.956
So I want, like,
an old-lady pop star.

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:11.224
-Yeah.

00:03:11.224 --> 00:03:12.958
-Okay, that's a middling
response, and I'm glad that I...

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:14.193
-But you would
have done a song.

00:03:14.193 --> 00:03:16.329
-I'm glad that I moved on.
It was a whole song.

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:17.596
You have to actually --
To get the context,

00:03:17.596 --> 00:03:18.765
you actually have
to hear the lyrics.

00:03:18.765 --> 00:03:20.867
But this is really good
feedback for me.

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:22.168
-But they don't understand.

00:03:22.168 --> 00:03:24.170
That's when -- You just laid out
a premise right there.

00:03:24.170 --> 00:03:25.738
I think it's a very
strong premise.

00:03:25.738 --> 00:03:28.207
-Yeah, but this is my unpaid
writers room, so thank you.

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:31.310
Uh-oh!
-Another strike!

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:35.481
-Now they're on strike!
-[ Chanting ] One more strike!

00:03:35.481 --> 00:03:38.651
-Yeah, so I was like,
"I can't do this same show now.

00:03:38.651 --> 00:03:41.087
The world has changed.
My life has changed."

00:03:41.087 --> 00:03:43.689
I'm in the middle of what was
my office with my newborn child.

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.024
I'm breastfeeding.

00:03:45.024 --> 00:03:48.260
My life is somewhat
of a shambles.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:49.629
And so that's
what inspired this show.

00:03:49.629 --> 00:03:52.198
I basically try to do the show
I wanted to do in 2020,

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:55.735
and then there is an interloper
in the form of death.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:58.104
-I want to say
I am always the most impressed

00:03:58.104 --> 00:04:01.908
and I do feel the best work
comes out of a creative person

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:03.743
who is willing to
throw away work.

00:04:03.743 --> 00:04:06.178
You know, because, again, once
those note cards are on the

00:04:06.178 --> 00:04:07.647
wall -- and I've
been there, too --

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.949
you can't stop thinking
about all the work

00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:10.983
you've already put into it,

00:04:10.983 --> 00:04:12.752
and it seems insane
to throw it away.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.120
Yet, anytime you talk
to somebody who tells you,

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:16.255
"Yeah, I knew it wasn't
the right stuff

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:17.389
and I threw it away,"

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:18.524
that's when the great thing
comes out of it.

00:04:18.524 --> 00:04:20.126
So I do applaud that choice.
-Thank you.

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:23.462
And what sucks is, I tried
to keep a lot of my old material

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:26.198
in the show,
and it all just lifted out.

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:27.433
And the one thing that's left,

00:04:27.433 --> 00:04:29.435
I can't say the title
on television,

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.838
but let's just say
it's about a certain tree

00:04:32.838 --> 00:04:36.442
that smells up
the entire country every year.

00:04:36.442 --> 00:04:37.877
And people call
this tree a thing.

00:04:37.877 --> 00:04:39.012
-Alright.

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:40.512
-It's a tree
that smells like semen.

00:04:40.512 --> 00:04:43.516
I think I can say semen, right?
Semen is scientific.

00:04:43.516 --> 00:04:45.150
-Yeah.
-It's the semen tree.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.952
-Okay, great.
-But I say another word for it.

00:04:46.952 --> 00:04:51.457
-I think everybody here
wants to come to this show now.

00:04:51.457 --> 00:04:53.125
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:53.125 --> 00:04:57.930
I got you.

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.866
You know, you talk about
death as a theme and you talk --

00:05:00.866 --> 00:05:05.104
There's a story about your dog
not passing away,

00:05:05.104 --> 00:05:07.573
but you received
a very interesting letter.

00:05:07.573 --> 00:05:09.375
Can you explain the story here?
-Yeah.

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.577
So, we got a business manager
a couple years ago,

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:14.447
and it's someone who kind of
looks over your finances,

00:05:14.447 --> 00:05:16.816
because I am financially
incapable of anything.

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.851
And he said,
"Oh, you have a dog.

00:05:18.851 --> 00:05:20.152
I'm going to put you
on something

00:05:20.152 --> 00:05:21.487
called pet health insurance."

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.790
And then a week later
he said, "Ah, you know what?

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.124
I found you a cheaper
health insurance.

00:05:25.124 --> 00:05:26.259
I switched you to that."

00:05:26.259 --> 00:05:27.893
So, unbeknownst to us,
the way he got us

00:05:27.893 --> 00:05:29.862
out of the first
pet health insurance was

00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:31.397
he told the company
our dog died.

00:05:31.397 --> 00:05:32.431
-Yeah.

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:34.300
-So, one day,
I'm walking my dog,

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:38.003
and then I come home and I open
up the mail to find this.

00:05:38.003 --> 00:05:39.037
-Yeah. Right.

00:05:39.037 --> 00:05:41.940
-Yeah, so,
"With deepest sympathy."

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.342
If you open up
that card there, Seth --

00:05:43.342 --> 00:05:44.443
-A lot of people
did a lot of work.

00:05:44.443 --> 00:05:48.381
-Yeah. So, this is
a condolence card

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:50.783
signed by everyone
who worked at this,

00:05:50.783 --> 00:05:52.685
turns out,
mom-and-pop insurance company.

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:54.253
-Yeah.

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.822
-So, I am looking at this card
and I am now being told

00:05:56.822 --> 00:05:59.759
that a dog that I've been
walking for the past half-hour

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:00.860
is, in fact, dead.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:02.395
Yeah.

00:06:02.395 --> 00:06:04.430
-And it messed
with my head for a while.

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:05.531
And this is the real card.

00:06:05.531 --> 00:06:07.734
And I have it laminated
in my drawer,

00:06:07.734 --> 00:06:09.601
and I take it out
and look at it a lot.

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:11.937
-Well, the good news is,
you you saved 20 bucks a month.

00:06:11.937 --> 00:06:15.141
So there you go.
[ Laughter ]

00:06:15.141 --> 00:06:18.577
You were very kind to bring --
You know, again,

00:06:18.577 --> 00:06:20.145
I think it's very helpful
when people talk about

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:22.114
that creative process
and looking things on the wall

00:06:22.114 --> 00:06:23.382
and realizing it's not right.

00:06:23.382 --> 00:06:26.085
And also early performance.
-Yes.

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:29.088
-You have some photos of some of
your early times in New York.

00:06:29.088 --> 00:06:31.790
And most of these, I would
imagine when you look at them,

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:33.492
your memory is of the anxiety.

00:06:33.492 --> 00:06:34.860
Is that safe to say?
-Oh, no.

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:36.128
You know, I actually have
really good memories of my time

00:06:36.128 --> 00:06:37.230
performing in New York.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:38.630
Oh, yeah, this is so great.

00:06:38.630 --> 00:06:41.801
So, this is a picture of me
on my college sketch group.

00:06:41.801 --> 00:06:43.969
I'm in a sketch with my friend
Matt and I'm on a date.

00:06:43.969 --> 00:06:45.103
It was so fun.

00:06:45.103 --> 00:06:46.538
What you don't see here is,
at the time,

00:06:46.538 --> 00:06:48.474
I was in a secret relationship
with a man

00:06:48.474 --> 00:06:50.009
who was ashamed to be with me.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:53.912
And this was the latest
in a continuing pattern

00:06:53.912 --> 00:06:57.249
of going for unavailable men
who were ashamed of me.

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:59.084
And so part of the reason

00:06:59.084 --> 00:07:01.420
I'm so happy in this sketch
is because, for once,

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:03.188
I can be openly in love
with someone,

00:07:03.188 --> 00:07:04.523
even if it's just pretend.

00:07:04.523 --> 00:07:07.560
-That's great.
-Good times.

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.696
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:10.696 --> 00:07:11.830
Oh, my gosh.

00:07:11.830 --> 00:07:13.232
-Now, what were you
going through here?

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:14.400
-This brings up such nostalgia.

00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:18.304
So, obviously here,
I'm dressed as a bear.

00:07:18.304 --> 00:07:20.839
I was the most unhappy
I'd ever been in my life.

00:07:20.839 --> 00:07:23.809
So, I found out later
that I have

00:07:23.809 --> 00:07:25.845
generalized anxiety disorder,
but at this time,

00:07:25.845 --> 00:07:28.147
it had kind of pendulumed to
general depression.

00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:30.016
And the secret relationship
was over,

00:07:30.016 --> 00:07:32.718
but that guy was going to be
at the sketch show this night.

00:07:32.718 --> 00:07:34.119
So I was like,
"I have to look hot."

00:07:34.119 --> 00:07:37.323
Now, luckily, my depression made
me kind of not want to eat,

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:39.658
so I was very skinny,
so I did look hot

00:07:39.658 --> 00:07:41.294
despite the fact
I was dressed as a bear.

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:44.130
-Yeah. It's a good memory there.

00:07:44.130 --> 00:07:46.065
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.567
-It's just such good --
It's just like nostalgia bath.

00:07:48.567 --> 00:07:51.170
-Yeah.
-Oh, right.

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.438
Okay, this is my first time
doing stand-up.

00:07:53.438 --> 00:07:56.476
Don't worry. The secret
relationship was over.

00:07:56.476 --> 00:07:58.610
But I will say that
at this moment,

00:07:58.610 --> 00:08:00.446
the OCD intrusive thoughts
that I had had

00:08:00.446 --> 00:08:02.782
as a kid reared their head again
in adulthood.

00:08:02.782 --> 00:08:04.717
And so as I was doing
this first stand-up set,

00:08:04.717 --> 00:08:07.186
it felt as if my body
was separate from my mind.

00:08:07.186 --> 00:08:08.821
But I still killed.
-Alright, great.

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:10.456
That's all that matters.

00:08:10.456 --> 00:08:12.424
In the end, our desperate need
to please strangers.

00:08:12.424 --> 00:08:15.694
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
New York comedy -- so fun.

00:08:15.694 --> 00:08:16.695
-How old is
your little one now?

00:08:16.695 --> 00:08:18.296
-She's 3 1/2.
-3 1/2.

00:08:18.296 --> 00:08:20.666
You said something
that very much rang true for me,

00:08:20.666 --> 00:08:23.802
which is, when you have a child,
especially that first child,

00:08:23.802 --> 00:08:26.772
you feel as though
you were cosplaying as a parent.

00:08:26.772 --> 00:08:28.240
-Yeah, yeah. The first --
I don't know about you.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:29.875
The first year
and a half I had a kid,

00:08:29.875 --> 00:08:31.810
I kept thinking,
"When are these people

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:33.145
going to come
and pick up their baby?"

00:08:33.145 --> 00:08:34.547
-Yeah.

00:08:34.547 --> 00:08:36.515
-Like, "Someone
needs to take her."

00:08:36.515 --> 00:08:39.652
And then now I say things --
Well, first of all,

00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:40.953
and you've talked about this
in your stand-up,

00:08:40.953 --> 00:08:42.388
just with the voice of like,
"Wow!

00:08:42.388 --> 00:08:44.690
Those are some big feelings."

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:46.992
You know, when she says
just the worst things to you,

00:08:46.992 --> 00:08:49.161
which you never -- If someone
cuts you off in traffic,

00:08:49.161 --> 00:08:50.796
you wouldn't pull them over
and be like, "Hey,

00:08:50.796 --> 00:08:53.699
you have some big feelings
right there.

00:08:53.699 --> 00:08:54.800
Do you want to talk about them?

00:08:54.800 --> 00:08:56.369
Do you want to watch
'Daniel Tiger'?"

00:08:56.369 --> 00:08:59.371
And then the way
that I sometimes speak to her

00:08:59.371 --> 00:09:00.839
are ways
that I would never speak to --

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:02.341
"Hey, do not!

00:09:02.341 --> 00:09:03.976
We don't pull the dog's tail.
We do not.

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:05.544
We don't pull any dog's tail."

00:09:05.544 --> 00:09:09.582
Like, I couldn't -- I would be
sued if I did that at work.

00:09:09.582 --> 00:09:11.516
But it just -- It feels like
you're doing an impression

00:09:11.516 --> 00:09:12.585
of a parent sometimes.

00:09:12.585 --> 00:09:15.287
-Yeah. Finally, we showed
a clip of "Julia"

00:09:15.287 --> 00:09:17.690
that's sort of, like,
a period-piece show

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:18.791
about Julia Child.

00:09:18.791 --> 00:09:20.292
Do you enjoy that?

00:09:20.292 --> 00:09:22.094
-I love it.
I thought it was so fun.

00:09:22.094 --> 00:09:25.231
My family's from Boston,
and so I filmed it in Boston.

00:09:25.231 --> 00:09:26.598
And it takes place
in the early '60s,

00:09:26.598 --> 00:09:28.734
so it kind of felt
like I was cosplaying

00:09:28.734 --> 00:09:31.503
as someone who knew my family.

00:09:31.503 --> 00:09:33.305
That was a callback to earlier.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:09:33.305 --> 00:09:37.209
-And what I find
kind of relieving is,

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:38.510
when you were in the '60s,

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:40.946
you only got -- Women got
their hair done once a week,

00:09:40.946 --> 00:09:43.148
and then your only job
was to maintain.

00:09:43.148 --> 00:09:44.416
And we think of
the '60s as this,

00:09:44.416 --> 00:09:46.384
oh, restrictive time for women.

00:09:46.384 --> 00:09:47.886
But how easy is that?

00:09:47.886 --> 00:09:50.422
We didn't have to look at TikTok
mermaid-wave tutorials.

00:09:50.422 --> 00:09:52.491
You got your hair done
once a week.

00:09:52.491 --> 00:09:55.794
And then, similarly, there's all
this pressure to just,

00:09:55.794 --> 00:09:58.296
"Oh, you got to work out
and have a skinny body."

00:09:58.296 --> 00:10:00.900
But that's what
a girdle was for.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:02.501
And that's why Spanx
are so popular.

00:10:02.501 --> 00:10:04.070
They're basically girdles.

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:06.972
Girdles have come back because
women have always wanted --

00:10:06.972 --> 00:10:09.207
We've always wanted to
smooth out our lumps, right?

00:10:09.207 --> 00:10:11.510
And, so, it's a lot easier
to wear a girdle,

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:15.147
to wear shapewear than to wish
for a different body type.

00:10:15.147 --> 00:10:17.616
So I found it actually
very empowering

00:10:17.616 --> 00:10:19.685
to wear the shapewear
and have my hair done.

00:10:19.685 --> 00:10:22.187
I felt like I could focus
on other things

00:10:22.187 --> 00:10:24.489
because someone else
was doing it for me.

00:10:24.489 --> 00:10:27.693
-Well, there you go.
That's a ringing endorsement.

00:10:27.693 --> 00:10:28.995
A ringing endorsement
of the times.

00:10:28.995 --> 00:10:30.295
-Yeah.

00:10:30.295 --> 00:10:32.164
-It is always such a delight
to have you back here.

00:10:32.164 --> 00:10:33.299
Thank you so much.
Congratulations on your show.

00:10:33.299 --> 00:10:34.834
-Thank you.

00:10:34.834 --> 00:10:37.002
-You guys, that's Rachel Bloom,
everybody.

00:10:37.002 --> 00:10:39.939
"Death, Let Me Do My Show"
opens at the Orpheum Theatre

00:10:39.939 --> 00:10:41.307
on December 7th,

00:10:41.307 --> 00:10:43.909
and new episodes of "Julia"
premiere Thursdays on Max.

00:10:43.909 --> 00:10:44.943
We'll be right back
with music from

00:10:44.943 --> 00:10:46.112
Michael Shannon
and Jason Narducy.

